    (
        16,
        31,
        "",
        "Jezebel the daughter of Ethbaal",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>Jezebel of Tyre.</strong> Daughter of Ethbaal (Ittobaal I, c. 887–856 BCE), king-priest of Sidon. Her name <em>Izevel</em> is the subject of probable scribal mockery — the original may have been theophoric (compare Phoenician <em>I-Ba\'al</em>, "where is Baal") and was likely revocalized to suggest <em>zevel</em> ("dung") or <em>ze\'evel</em> ("not exalted"). She brings Tyrian Baal-worship to the highest level of Israelite politics — 450 prophets of Baal eating at her table (18:19). Her arc through 1 Kgs 16 – 2 Kgs 9 is the spine of the Elijah/Elisha narrative complex.',
        "User original",
    ),
