        "Hebrew",
        "Hebrew",
        '<strong>Qol demamah daqqah (<em>קוֹל</em> <em>דְּמָמָה</em> <em>דַקָּה</em>).</strong> Notoriously hard to translate. <em>Demamah</em> means "stillness" or "silence"; <em>daqqah</em> means "thin," "fine," "minute." Literally: "the sound of a thin silence" — a paradoxical phrase, a noise that is silence. KJV’s "still small voice" is beautiful but tames the strangeness; some moderns translate "sheer silence" (NRSV) or "a soft murmuring sound." The theophany inverts Sinai’s thunder, fire, and earthquake (Exod 19) — Yahweh is precisely <em>not</em> in those manifestations here. The verse is a foundational text for apophatic and contemplative traditions across Judaism, Christianity, and Islam.',
        "User original",
